java-project-layout
===================

Java project layout from simple to comlicate and why that complicate?

00-no-structure
---------------
Just single Java file. Simple but does not scale (scale = how well the project handles growth - more files, more developers, more features)
```
java Hello.java
```
* 1 Java file
* compile/run the file


01-some-files
-------------
A few Java files seperating concerns. Needs to compile
```
javac Hello.java
java Hello
```
* multiple files
* compile sources (by compiling the entrypoint all other files will be compiled too)
* run the entrypoint (the class containing the main method)


02-source-dest-folders
----------------------
Adding the idea of source and dest folders. Avoid possible collision with produced artifacts.
Side:
* javac -d <dir>    | where to put the compiled .class files
* javac -cp <path>  | where to look for already-compiled classes your code depends on
* java -cp <path>   | where to look for .class files when running
```
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Butler.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Hello.java
java -cp bin Hello
```


03-package-as-jar
-----------------
The java way to distribute programs.
Side:
* jar c         | create new JAR file
* jar v         | Verbose - print out what's being added
* jar f         | File - the next argument is the JAR filename to create
* jar -C bin    | Change into the bin/ directory before adding files
* jar .         | Add everything in that directory
```
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Butler.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Hello.java
jar cvf target/lonely.jar -C bin . 
java -cp target/lonely.jar Hello
```


04-managing-dependencies
------------------------
(too lazy to type it out, if you need the code, just go to https://github.com/sombriks/how-to-structure-java-projects)
Using code from others, stand on the shoulders of giants.
Example using Gson lib to parse json data.
```
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Item.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Quotes.java
javac -d bin -cp bin:lib/gson-2.10.1.jar src/Grievous.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/HelloThere.java
jar cvfe target/star-wars-2.jar HelloThere -C bin . 
java -cp lib/gson-2.10.1.jar:target/star-wars-2.jar HelloThere
```
Alternative way is using MANIFEST.MF
```
Manifest-Version: 1.0
Main-Class: HelloThere
Class-Path: gson-2.10.1.jar


```
so the command can be like this
```
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Item.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/Quotes.java
javac -d bin -cp bin:lib/gson-2.10.1.jar src/Grievous.java
javac -d bin -cp bin src/HelloThere.java
jar cvfm target/star-wars-2.jar src/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF -C bin .
cp lib/gson-2.10.1.jar target/gson-2.10.1.jar
java -jar target/star-wars-2.jar
```
but this is too much effort for just one dependency, having hundreds of them will be problems


05-ant-project
--------------
higher level project configuration for java projects.
ant + build.xml...
I just dont care, I will go to maven


06-maven-project
----------------
okay, now it is a mess, and I understand why we should use IDE for simple :)



Conclusion
==========
From single file to maven, we can agree that maven is a mess :), the only concept would be understand is we seperate folder into it function like src -> code base, bin -> binary file, target -> jar file, and some config file for maven.



